===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[cross]] shaped like a T with a loop at the top, the Egyptian [[hieroglyph]] representing the Egyptian [[triliteral]] {{l|egy|ꜥnḫ|gloss=life}} and often used as an [[amulet]] or charm for this concept.
#* '''1994''', Mira Bartok, Christine Ronan, ''Ancient Egypt and Nubia'', [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=Hh702TD97S0C&pg=PP13&dq=%22ankh%22%7C%22ankhs%22&hl=en&sa=X&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22ankh%22%7C%22ankhs%22&f=false page 11],
#*: On temple wall paintings, the '''ankh''' is often seen being carried by gods who hold it up to the nose of the pharaoh. This gesture symbolizes the offering of the "breath of life" or eternity. Sometimes the pharaoh is also depicted with a stream of water in the form of '''ankhs''' being poured over him during a purification ritual.
#* '''2001''', Anna Franklin, Paul Mason, ''Lammas: Celebrating Fruits of the First Harvest'', [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=oH77QJewHQIC&pg=PA145&dq=%22ankh%22%7C%22ankhs%22&hl=en&sa=X&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22ankh%22%7C%22ankhs%22&f=false page 245],
#*: The '''ankh''' combines the male and female sexual symbols: a female oval surmounting a male cross. It was also called the Key of the Nile, and was said to represent the sacred marriage between the God and Goddess which took place each year at the source of the Nile before the river flooded. Egyptians regarded the '''ankh''' as a universal life charm.
#* '''2013''', Gordon Strong, ''Windleroot'', [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=7eb0AAAAQBAJ&pg=PA125&dq=%22ankh%22%7C%22ankhs%22&hl=en&sa=X&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22ankh%22%7C%22ankhs%22&f=false page 125],
#*: Now, the '''ankh''' that Thoth always held hovered in the air between Sekhmet and the Magus, and both fixed their gaze upon it.
#  tau cross

